in general I get a screenplay maybe six  weeks or six months ahead of time and I  hope that I'm able to make some notes  and give the director of those notes  sometimes I've done that and the  director says I don't like these notes  and then we part early and that's good  because that's a way of sort of testing  the water before you actually dive in  but in general the the notes are  received with interest sometimes they  actually do some of the things that I  suggest but that's not a criteria it's  just a way for me to start getting into  the story by writing by thinking about  it already in writing notes I also time  the screenplay which not all editors do  I would recommend it because it's  another way of sort of fitting yourself  inside the suit of this film you to time  a screenplay accurately you have to do  it two times at least and to do that you  actually have to visualize everything  that's on the page many problems I think  in films come from the fact that  something hasn't been thoroughly  visualized the some quark anyway but  that's another thing that I do to help  get purchase on this material yeah I  have something that's called the rule of  six that that's more about actually when  you're doing the editing it was an  attempt to answer the question in some  ideal let's say even platonic world what  is a perfect cut from one shot to  another the strange thing about editing  is  and cutting from one shot to another is  that before we actually started doing it  which was only a hundred and twenty  years ago it might not have worked it's  the idea of actually splicing two  different shots together and having the  audience accept that as a continuous  reality is surprising it's surprising  that it works so this was a hit list  really of criteria and the top criteria  was emotion which is very difficult to  quantify or qualify even how did how  does this transition from one shot to  another make you feel you have to keep  your fingers on that emotional pulse as  an editor and not really ever let it go  you your grip sometimes loosens a little  bit but it's like riding a horse you  have to always hold the emotional reins  in your hand sometimes you actually have  to jerk it a little bit but hopefully  not too often after that is story does  it help tell the story after that is  rhythm and you can think of shots cut  together as a kind of rhythm track the  syncopation of the cut which is a big  shift I mean the cut lots of things  change at that point and that rhythm has  to integrate itself within the rhythm of  the actors and the rhythm of the  camerawork other other things that are  determining the rhythm of the shot and  then there are three slightly less  significant things which you have to  have a good sense of where the audience  is looking in the frame because they're  not looking at the whole frame all the  time they're choosing to look at areas  and that determines how good a cut is  and then the so-called 180 degree rule  does it look like two people are talking  to each other when do you violate that  when do you say  I don't care I'm working on a  documentary at the moment and  documentaries for the most part let go  of that the least-significant you can  tell I'm going kind of down in  significance the last thing is what you  might call three-dimensional continuity  which is did that person get up from the  chair and walk across the room in a  convincing way or was it confusing where  they are in the early days of film that  they paid a lot of attention to that and  you can look at the films stylistically  over the 20th century and you can see  that we've let that wander well we were  all film students Francis actually was  at UCLA and he was four years older so  he was I think he'd already graduated by  the time I was in film school George  Lucas was also going to USC as I was and  it was hopeless to think that we could  work in Hollywood and we were told this  off over and over by those teachers in  fact the the first thing that the  teacher said on the very first minute of  the first day was you can still get your  money back you can get out now and go do  something that will earn you a living  because there's no jobs for you 1965  which is when this was was and still is  the low point of Hollywood production  fewer films were made then than ever  since and the teachers who were all 20  years older than us saw this slope  clearly and they thought it was just  going to keep going and so they sort of  pitied us why are you doing that when  Francis and George and I started  American zoetrope up in San Francisco  the mood was let's get out of this  Hollywood thing and make films the way  we made student films we'll just make  them bigger that was the dream and it  went through many rocky period  and films that didn't commercially  anyway succeed until the Godfather and  that was the first big film that that we  all worked on and that became a big hit  Godfather was the first time that I'd  ever worked in Hollywood in the the  bowels of the machine so to speak and I  took chances and discovered that in fact  even though it's this big machine you  can actually do these tricky things and  it's the scene where Michael kills so  also in the restaurant which happens  about midway through the film and  Francis and Nino Rota had decided to  have no music throughout the whole scene  and the other unusual thing was that it  was a fairly long scene the restaurant  scene and a lot of it was in Italian  with no subtitles I mean that's a risky  thing to do today let alone 40 something  years ago so it threw the audience a  curveball  they didn't quite you know what's going  on there so there's no music which  usually guides you and you don't  understand what they're talking about so  you have to pay a lot of attention to a  body language and expression and the  quality of the voice but I thought well  let's do something with sound that might  help the moment and because I grew up 